<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jan Hus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1370–<a href="/years/1415">1415</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as John Hus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jan Hus was a Czech priest and reformer, rector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-university">University of Prague</a>, whose criticism of church abuses drew on the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wycliffe">John Wycliffe</a>. Summoned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> under a safe-conduct from King Sigismund, he refused to recant and was burned at the stake there in July 1415. His execution provoked the Hussite movement and wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and he was later seen as a forerunner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jan-hus.jpg" alt="Jan Hus" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christoph Murer 1587 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stimmer_Jan_Hus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jan Hus was born at Husinec in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> to peasant parents; the exact date is disputed and most of what is known of his early life is unsubstantiated. He took his name from the village, and the word hus means goose in Czech. He began teaching at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-university">University of Prague</a> in 1398, defended propositions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wycliffe">John Wycliffe</a> in public for the first time in 1399, and was ordained as a Catholic priest in 1400. He became dean of the philosophy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> in 1401 and a rector of the university a year later, and in 1402 he was appointed preacher at the Bethlehem Chapel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hus translated Wycliffe's Trialogus into Czech and helped distribute it, even though Church authorities banned many of Wycliffe's works in 1403. From his pulpit he denounced the moral failings of clergy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and even the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. In 1409 King Wenceslaus IV, wishing the university to stay neutral in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Western Schism</a>, decreed that the Czech nation should have three votes and the German nation only one. The German masters and students left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, more than a thousand of them by one estimate, and spread accusations of Bohemian heresy. Hus was excommunicated and spent two years in exile.</p><p>King Sigismund arranged a general council at Constance, which opened on 1 November 1414, and Hus agreed to attend under his promise of safe-conduct. At his trial on 8 June 1415 thirty-nine sentences drawn from his books were read to him. He declared himself willing to recant if his errors were proved from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, but refused to confess that he had erred. He was burned at the stake on 6 July 1415, and his ashes were thrown into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 2 September 1415 a hundred nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> sealed a protest against his burning. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-zizka">Jan Žižka</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prokop-the-great">Prokop the Great</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> sent against them, and the fighting ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compacts-of-basel">Basel Compacts</a> of 1436, which allowed Bohemia to practise its own form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. A century later as much as ninety per cent of the Czech lands' inhabitants still followed Hussite teaching. In 1999 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> expressed deep regret for the cruel death inflicted on Hus. His followers survive in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Church</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jan Hus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Jan Hus</b>, a Prague preacher influenced by the writings of John Wycliffe, attacked church abuses and was burned at the Council of Constance in 1415.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>For this reason there was neither a Czech liturgy nor communion under both kinds when <b>Jan Hus</b> proposed those reforms in the 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/movement/hussites">Hussites · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The followers of John Wycliffe in England were driven underground, and <b>Jan Hus</b> was burned as a heretic at the Council of Constance in 1415, although his followers kept a church of their own in Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In the first of the three Defenestrations of Prague, a crowd of radical Hussites, followers of the Czech reformer <b>Jan Hus</b>, threw members of the city council from a window, killing seven.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-30">30 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Czech reformer <b>Jan Hus</b>, condemned as a heretic by the Council of Constance despite a promise of safe conduct, was burned.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-6">6 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Jerome of Prague, a Czech philosopher and theologian and one of the chief followers of <b>Jan Hus</b>, was burned for heresy at the Council of Constance.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-30">30 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Husinec, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Constance</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.06,13.99&amp;year=1399" aria-label="Husinec on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4873 452 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jan Hus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5323 722h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5323 722h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.06,13.99&amp;year=1399">What was here in 1399? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
